          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following complications is specific to monochorionic twin pregnanci</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following complications is specific to monochorionic twin pregnancies rather than being a general risk of all multiple pregnancies?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Preterm labour</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Preterm labour is a general complication of ALL multiple pregnancies resulting from increased uterine distension. It is not specific to monochorionic twins.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Post-partum haemorrhage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>PPH is a general complication of all multiple pregnancies, driven by the over-distended uterus and larger placental site. It occurs regardless of chorionicity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>TTTS is specific to monochorionic twins because it requires shared placental vascular anastomoses. Unbalanced blood flow through these anastomoses creates a donor–recipient imbalance — pathophysiology impossible in dichorionic twins with separate placentas.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Maternal anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Anaemia affects all multiple pregnancies because the mother must sustain greater red cell mass and iron demands for multiple fetuses, regardless of whether they share a placenta.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "TTTS is specific to monochorionic twins. Complications like anaemia, preterm labour, and PPH affect all multiple pregnancies regardless of chorionicity, whereas TTTS requires the shared placental circulation unique to monochorionic twins."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! TTTS is specific to monochorionic twins whose shared placenta contains inter-twin vascular anastomoses, distinguishing it from general complications like anaemia, preterm labour, and PPH."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
