          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>An MCDA twin pregnancy is identified at 12 weeks. According to chorionicity-stra</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>An MCDA twin pregnancy is identified at 12 weeks. According to chorionicity-stratified surveillance guidelines, at what gestational age should fortnightly ultrasound scans begin?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>20 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Starting at 20 weeks delays surveillance by 4 critical weeks. TTTS can develop rapidly through shared placental anastomoses, and earlier detection from 16 weeks improves outcomes with timely intervention.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>16 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MCDA twins require fortnightly surveillance from 16 weeks per NICE NG137 guidelines. This intensive early monitoring is essential because TTTS can develop within days, and timely detection enables intervention such as fetoscopic laser ablation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>24 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Waiting until 24 weeks would miss the early second-trimester window for TTTS detection. The recommended start for fortnightly MCDA surveillance is 16 weeks.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>28 weeks</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Twenty-eight weeks is the gestation at which MCMA twins (not MCDA) require inpatient admission due to additional cord entanglement risk. MCDA fortnightly surveillance begins much earlier, at 16 weeks.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Fortnightly surveillance for MCDA twins begins at 16 weeks. This is part of the chorionicity-stratified approach: DCDA twins receive 4-weekly scans with delivery at 38 weeks, while MCDA twins need intensive monitoring from 16 weeks with delivery at 36–37 weeks."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct! MCDA twins require fortnightly surveillance from 16 weeks, with planned delivery at 36–37 weeks — reflecting the higher risk from shared placentation."
                }
              ],
